   "cite": "fs-id1167794040017",
   "prompt": "Find the domain and range of the function $f(x,y)=\\sqrt{36-9x^{2}-9y^{2}}.$",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "<p>The domain is the shaded circle defined by the inequality $9x^{2}+9y^{2}\\le 36,$ which has a circle of radius $2$ as its boundary. The range is $[0,6].$</p>\n<img src=\"/media/ch14/CNX_Calc_Figure_14_01_003.jpg\" alt=\"A circle of radius two with center at the origin. The equation x2 + y2 ≤ 4 is given.\">",
    "hint": "Determine the set of ordered pairs that do not make the radicand negative."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cp2",
   "source_id": "fs-id1167793964400",
   "source_number": "Checkpoint 14.2",
   "kind": "checkpoint",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "inline",
    "after": "fs-id1167793961239"
   },
   "cite": "fs-id1167793882806",
   "prompt": "Find and graph the level curve of the function $g(x,y)=x^{2}+y^{2}-6x+2y$ corresponding to $c=15.$",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "<p>The equation of the level curve can be written as ${(x-3)}^{2}+{(y+1)}^{2}=25,$ which is a circle with radius $5$ centered at $(3,-1).$</p>\n<img src=\"/media/ch14/CNX_Calc_Figure_14_01_011.jpg\" alt=\"An circle of radius 5 with center (3, –1).\">",
    "hint": "First, set $g(x,y)=15$ and then complete the square."
   }
  },
  {
   "id": "cp3",
   "source_id": "fs-id1167793638040",
   "source_number": "Checkpoint 14.3",
   "kind": "checkpoint",
   "bloom": "Apply",
   "place": {
    "at": "inline",
    "after": "fs-id1167793291691"
   },
   "cite": "fs-id1167793285160",
   "prompt": "Determine the equation of the vertical trace of the function $g(x,y)=-x^{2}-y^{2}+2x+4y-1$ corresponding to $y=3,$ and describe its graph.",
   "answer": {
    "type": "open",
    "generated_by": "source",
    "solution": "$z=3-{(x-1)}^{2}.$ This function describes a parabola opening downward in the plane $y=3.$",
    "hint": "Set $y=3$ in the equation $z=-x^{2}-y^{2}+2x+4y-1$ and complete the square."
   }
  },
  {
