   "cite": "fs-id1167794040017",
   "prompt": "<p>For the following exercises, determine whether the statement is <em>true or false</em>. Justify your answer with a proof or a counterexample.</p><p>The domain of $f(x,y)=x^{3}{\\sin }^{-1}(y)$ is $x=$ all real numbers, and $-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi .$</p>",
   "answer": {
    "type": "choice",
    "options": [
     "True",
     "False"
    ],
    "correct": 1,
    "solution": "False. The factor $x^{3}$ is defined for all real numbers, so $x$ is unrestricted, but $\\sin^{-1}(y)$ is defined only for $-1\\le y\\le 1,$ the range of the sine function. The domain is $\\{(x,y)\\in \\mathbb{R}^{2}\\mid -1\\le y\\le 1\\},$ not $-\\pi \\le y\\le \\pi$: for example, $y=2$ lies in $[-\\pi ,\\pi ]$ but $\\sin^{-1}(2)$ does not exist.",
    "generated_by": "ai"
   }
  }
